\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The induced scalar plaquette coefficient}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{On a square torus with both periods at least 6, take a real
scalar field and #{G=\{1,-1\}} in its sign representation.
Use unit weights, #{m>0}, #{N=|\Lambda|} and #{D=4+m^2}.
For the \vocabk{plaquette holonomies}{fdip-006N},
##{\begin{aligned}
\cW(U)={}&C(D,N)-\frac1{D^4}\sum_pU_p+R(U),\\
C(D,N)={}&\frac N2\log D-\frac N{D^2}-\frac{7N}{2D^4},\\
|R(U)|\le{}&\frac N{12}\frac{(4/D)^6}{1-4/D}.
\end{aligned}}
Thus the fourth-order induced Wilson coefficient is
#{\beta_{\mathrm{ind}}=(4+m^2)^{-4}>0}. The remainder is uniformly
#{O(D^{-6})} in the connection at fixed finite volume as
#{D\to\infty}.}

\proof{
\p{A torus walk of length at most 5 lifts to a walk in
#{\mathbb Z^2} whose displacement in each coordinate is a multiple
of the corresponding period. Each displacement has absolute value
at most 5, so both must be zero. Such a walk has even length.
Consequently
##{\trace H_U=\trace H_U^3
 =\trace H_U^5=0.}
There are 4 two-step returns at each vertex, each traversing one
edge and its inverse, so #{\trace H_U^2=4N}.}

\p{There are 36 four-step returns at each vertex of
#{\mathbb Z^2}. Of them, 12 use one axis: on either axis there are 6
orderings of two positive and two negative steps. The other 24 use
both axes: these are the permutations of the 4 distinct directions.
Of those, 8 trace elementary squares; the other 16 retrace
edges. All 28 retracing walks have holonomy one.
Each plaquette has 4 choices of starting vertex and two orientations,
which give the same sign #{U_p}. Hence
##{\trace H_U^4=28N+8\sum_pU_p.}
Insert these traces into the [closed-walk series](fdip-0071), with
#{\kappa=1/2}, and use its remainder bound with #{K=5}.}
}

\p{The exact matter factor can therefore be written
##{e^{-\cW(U)}=e^{-C(D,N)}
 \exp\left(\beta_{\mathrm{ind}}\sum_pU_p\right)e^{-R(U)}.}
The displayed plaquette term favors positive holonomy. Higher closed
walks remain in #{R}; the complete determinant is not replaced by a
pure plaquette action. Winding walks of length 6 are permitted by
the hypothesis and belong to the remainder. On an odd period such as
7, odd winding walks also occur at higher orders; the proof only
asserts vanishing through length 5.}
